# tools/r/rye_toolchain_resolve_witness.rish -- the compiler finds the toolchain this tree ships.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `tools/f/fetch-toolchain.sh` is the second command a newcomer runs, and it
# installs a pinned Zig 0.16.0 to `vendor/zig-toolchain/zig` against a checksum this repository
# holds. `rye build` then spawned a toolchain resolved from two places and neither was that one:
# an explicit RYE_ZIG, else a bare `zig` for the host's PATH to answer. On a host with no `zig` on
# PATH -- this pier -- the tree's own compiler refused to build the tree, and its refusal named
# two doors while the one the newcomer had just walked through went unmentioned.
#
# THE DISAGREEMENT WAS INSIDE ONE DIRECTORY. `rye/bootstrap.sh` has read
# `${RYE_ZIG:-../vendor/zig-toolchain/zig}` since it was written -- the cold start knew where the
# toolchain lived and the compiler it bootstraps did not. One question, two answers, and only the
# cold start's was right. The tree's witnesses had all worked around it by hand: over a thousand
# tracked sources spell `RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig` into an `env` line, which is one rule
# written a thousand times.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal, over `tools/fixtures/r/rye_toolchain_resolve_control.sh`. Three
# readings in order -- an explicit RYE_ZIG, then the pinned toolchain found beside the binary at
# `<exe_dir>/../../vendor/zig-toolchain/zig`, then `zig` for PATH -- each shown from BOTH sides:
# the reading answers when it should, and the next one answers when it should not. The vendored
# reading is resolved against the binary's own location rather than the working directory, so a
# build from any directory finds the same toolchain. A candidate that is present and not
# executable falls through rather than spawning. The refusal names all three places and the
# command that installs the pinned one. The cold start and the compiler are read against each
# other and must name one destination.
#
# AND THREE MUTATIONS ARE REBUILT AND SHOWN TO BITE -- the vendored reading removed, RYE_ZIG moved
# out of first place, the executable check dropped -- since a leg that has never failed cannot be
# told from a leg that cannot fail.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. That the pinned toolchain is the right toolchain for a given host. A host
# `zig` stays reachable through RYE_ZIG and through the last reading; what this rung settles is
# that the tree's own answer is the one the compiler takes when nobody says otherwise.
#
# A BINARY OLDER THAN ITS SOURCE IS A MACHINE FACT, never a red: `rye/bin/rye` is gitignored, so a
# fresh clone holds none. The control asks the version at the door and skips by name.
#
# No network, no key, no funds, no device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/rye_toolchain_resolve_witness.rish

say "rye-toolchain-resolve: Language EN -- a compiler that cannot find the toolchain its own tree installs sends a newcomer looking for a door the tree already opened."
say "rye-toolchain-resolve: Style Gauge, Meter -- every reading is shown from both sides, and each of the three mutations names the leg it drops."
say "rye-toolchain-resolve: Lens TAME -- safety first: an explicit setting still wins, a candidate is asked whether it can be RUN before it is spawned, and every refusal falls through to the next reading rather than stopping."

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rye_toolchain_resolve_control.sh"]
say control.out
assert control.ok else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the resolver did not survive its own pen -- ${control.err}"

assert control.out contains "resolve_verdict=" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the control must name a verdict before it is believed"

if control.out contains "resolve_verdict=machine_fact" then say "rye-toolchain-resolve: SKIPPED on a machine fact -- this pier's rye binary predates the source it would speak for. Rebuild with 'sh rye/bootstrap.sh' and run this again; nothing in the tree is wrong."
if control.out contains "resolve_verdict=machine_fact" then exit 0

assert control.out contains "resolve_verdict=green" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the control must read green or name its machine fact"
assert control.out contains "CONTROL_GREEN" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the control must close on its own green line"
assert control.out contains "bootstrap and compiler agree on vendor/zig-toolchain" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the cold start and the compiler must name one toolchain destination"
assert control.out contains "three readings in order" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: the three readings must each be proven from both sides"
assert control.out contains "three mutations bitten" else "rye-toolchain-resolve: a leg that has never failed cannot be told from a leg that cannot fail"

say "GREEN: rye-toolchain-resolve -- rye reads RYE_ZIG, then the pinned toolchain beside its own binary, then PATH, and says so when none of the three answers."
